// Scenario-based quiz questions, batch a.
const m = (id, c, l, q, o, a, e) => ({ id, c, l, q, o, a, e });

export const MCQ_A = [
  // ---------- C# language and runtime ----------
  m("ca01", "csharp", "j",
    "A service method is declared `async void SendEmailAsync()` and an API action calls it without awaiting. The SMTP call inside throws. What is the most likely production effect?",
    ["The exception is stored in a Task and surfaces the next time the caller awaits that call",
     "The exception escapes the caller's try/catch and, if unhandled, can terminate the whole process",
     "ASP.NET Core catches it in the request pipeline and the request returns a 500 response",
     "The exception is swallowed silently because no Task exists to observe it"],
    1,
    "An `async void` method has no Task to hold the exception, so it is raised on the captured context or the thread pool, outside any try/catch the caller wrote; unhandled, it takes the process down. The silent-swallow belief comes from `Task`-returning methods with unobserved exceptions, which behave differently."),
  m("ca02", "csharp", "m",
    "A WPF button handler runs `var data = LoadAsync().Result;` and the UI freezes forever. `LoadAsync` awaits an `HttpClient` call internally. What is the cause?",
    ["`HttpClient` is not thread-safe and blocks when called from the UI thread",
     "`.Result` runs the task on a new thread that cannot touch UI controls",
     "The thread pool is exhausted, so the continuation never gets a worker thread",
     "The continuation must resume on the UI thread, but `.Result` is blocking that thread"],
    3,
    "The `await` captured the UI synchronization context, so its continuation is posted to the UI thread, which is blocked waiting for that very task: a classic sync-over-async deadlock. Thread pool starvation is a real sync-over-async problem in ASP.NET Core, but it does not explain a freeze with a single call."),
  m("ca03", "csharp", "m",
    "`await Task.WhenAll(a, b, c)` is used, and tasks `a` and `b` both fail with different exceptions. The catch block logs `ex.Message` and only one error appears. Why?",
    ["`await` rethrows only the first exception; all of them remain in the `WhenAll` task's `Exception.InnerExceptions`",
     "`WhenAll` completes as soon as the first task fails, so the second failure never happens",
     "`WhenAll` throws an `AggregateException` whose `Message` shows only the first inner message",
     "Task `c` finished last, and its successful result replaced the earlier failures"],
    0,
    "When you `await` a faulted task, the awaiter unwraps and throws just the first inner exception. Keep the `WhenAll` task in a variable and read `task.Exception` to see them all. `WhenAll` does wait for every task, so the second failure does occur."),
  m("ca04", "csharp", "s",
    "```public record Order(int Id, List<string> Tags);\nvar a = new Order(1, new() { \"vip\" });\nvar b = a with { Id = 2 };\nb.Tags.Add(\"late\");```\nWhat is `a.Tags.Count` afterwards?",
    ["1, because `with` makes a deep copy of the record",
     "Compile error, because record properties are init-only and `Tags` cannot change",
     "2, because `with` copies the reference, so both records share one list",
     "1, because `with` clones collection members but not other reference types"],
    2,
    "A `with` expression performs a shallow, member-wise copy, so both records point to the same `List<string>`. Init-only blocks reassigning `Tags`, but it does not stop you from mutating the list the property refers to."),
  m("ca05", "csharp", "m",
    "```var active = db.Users.Where(u => u.IsActive);\nif (active.Count() > 0)\n    foreach (var u in active) Notify(u);```\nThe profiler shows two SQL queries for this code. What explains it?",
    ["`Where` is eager, so the filter runs once for `Count()` and again for the loop",
     "The query is deferred and re-executes on every enumeration; materialize once with `ToList()`",
     "EF Core caches results per context, so the second query signals a framework bug",
     "`Notify` cannot be translated to SQL, so the loop forces a second server-side evaluation"],
    1,
    "An `IQueryable` is just a query definition; each `Count()` or `foreach` sends it to the database again, and the data may even differ between the two runs. Calling `ToList()` once and reusing the list fixes it, whereas EF does not cache query results between executions."),
  m("ca06", "csharp", "j",
    "```foreach (var item in cart.Items)\n    if (item.Qty == 0) cart.Items.Remove(item);```\nThis throws `InvalidOperationException` for some carts. What is the cleanest fix?",
    ["Use a `for` loop counting up from index 0 and call `RemoveAt(i)`",
     "Wrap the loop in `lock (cart.Items)`",
     "Change the declared type to `IEnumerable<T>` so the list cannot be modified",
     "Call `cart.Items.RemoveAll(i => i.Qty == 0)`"],
    3,
    "`List<T>` enumerators fail fast when the list changes during iteration; `RemoveAll` does the filtering in one pass. An upward `for` loop with `RemoveAt` avoids the exception but skips the element after each removal, and a lock does nothing for same-thread modification."),
  m("ca07", "csharp", "m",
    "A `Money` class overrides `Equals` to compare amount and currency but not `GetHashCode`. `hashSet.Contains(new Money(5, \"EUR\"))` sometimes returns false even though an equal item was added. Why?",
    ["The default `GetHashCode` is reference-based, so equal values land in different buckets and `Equals` is never consulted",
     "`HashSet<T>` looks items up through `IComparable<T>`, which `Money` does not implement",
     "`HashSet<T>` is not thread-safe, so the item was lost during a resize",
     "`Equals` must be declared `sealed` before hash-based collections will call it"],
    0,
    "Hash collections first use the hash code to pick a bucket and only then call `Equals`; equal objects must therefore return equal hash codes. Without the override, two equal instances usually hash differently, so lookups miss intermittently."),
  m("ca08", "csharp", "m",
    "A long-running service shows thousands of closed `OrderView` objects still alive in a memory profile. Each one subscribed with `PriceFeed.Instance.Updated += OnUpdated;` in its constructor, and `PriceFeed` is a singleton. What is the fix?",
    ["Call `GC.Collect()` whenever a view is closed",
     "Add a finalizer to `OrderView` that nulls its fields",
     "Unsubscribe with `-=` when the view is disposed, because the publisher's delegate list holds a strong reference",
     "Declare `OnUpdated` as `static` so the instance is not captured"],
    2,
    "The long-lived publisher keeps a delegate pointing at each subscriber, so the GC considers them reachable until they unsubscribe. A forced collection or finalizer cannot help for objects that are still rooted, and a static handler would lose access to the view's state."),
  m("ca09", "csharp", "j",
    "A console app has `<Nullable>enable</Nullable>`. It deserializes `{}` with System.Text.Json into `class Customer { public string Name { get; set; } = null!; }`. What is `customer.Name` at runtime?",
    ["An empty string, because non-nullable strings default to `\"\"`",
     "`null`; nullable reference annotations are compile-time only and the runtime does not enforce them",
     "The deserializer throws because `Name` is a non-nullable property",
     "The compiler's generated setter throws `ArgumentNullException`"],
    1,
    "Nullable reference types are static-analysis annotations; `= null!` just silences the warning and nothing validates the value when the JSON omits the property. This is why deserialized data can still be null despite non-nullable declarations."),
  m("ca10", "csharp", "m",
    "```enum Status { New, Paid, Shipped, Refunded }\nstring Label(Status s) => s switch {\n  Status.New => \"new\", Status.Paid => \"paid\", Status.Shipped => \"shipped\" };```\nThis compiles with a warning. A `Refunded` order reaches it in production. What happens?",
    ["It returns `null` because no arm matched",
     "It returns \"new\", the result of the first arm",
     "It returns \"shipped\", the last arm, by fall-through",
     "It throws `SwitchExpressionException` at runtime"],
    3,
    "A switch expression that is not exhaustive produces warning CS8509 at compile time, and if no arm matches at runtime it throws `SwitchExpressionException`. There is no fall-through or default result in switch expressions, so add the missing arm or treat the warning as an error."),
  m("ca11", "csharp", "s",
    "A cache returns `ValueTask<Product>`. A developer stores it in a variable and awaits it twice: `var t = cache.GetAsync(id); await t; ... await t;`. Under load it intermittently throws or returns wrong data. What is wrong?",
    ["A `ValueTask` must be consumed only once, as its backing object may be reused; call `AsTask()` to await it multiple times",
     "`ValueTask` is a struct, so the second await runs on a copy that never completes",
     "A `ValueTask` cannot be stored in a local variable and must be awaited in the same expression",
     "`ValueTask` completes synchronously, so the second await resumes on the wrong thread"],
    0,
    "When a `ValueTask` is backed by a pooled `IValueTaskSource`, awaiting it a second time (or concurrently) reads state that may already belong to another operation. If you need to await more than once, convert with `AsTask()` once. Storing it in a local is allowed."),
  m("ca12", "csharp", "m",
    "You maintain a NuGet library used by WPF, WinForms and ASP.NET Core apps. A WPF consumer deadlocks by calling your async API with `.Result`. Which library-side change reduces that risk?",
    ["Declare public async methods as `async void` so callers cannot block on them",
     "Use `ConfigureAwait(true)` explicitly so context capture is predictable",
     "Catch `AggregateException` inside the library and retry the call",
     "Use `ConfigureAwait(false)` on awaits so continuations do not need the caller's synchronization context"],
    3,
    "Library code rarely needs to resume on the caller's context, so `ConfigureAwait(false)` lets continuations run on the pool and removes the deadlock with blocked UI threads. `async void` would make the API unawaitable and unsafe, and `ConfigureAwait(true)` is just the default behavior."),
  m("ca13", "csharp", "s",
    "An API reads each upload into a fresh `new byte[200_000]`. GC counters show frequent Gen 2 collections and fragmentation under load. What is the most effective change?",
    ["Call `GC.Collect()` after each request to free the buffers promptly",
     "Rent buffers from `ArrayPool<byte>.Shared` and return them when done",
     "Switch to Workstation non-concurrent GC to make Gen 2 cheaper",
     "Wrap each buffer in a `WeakReference` so the GC reclaims it sooner"],
    1,
    "Arrays of about 85,000 bytes or more go to the Large Object Heap, which is only collected with Gen 2, so frequent large allocations cause expensive collections. Pooling avoids allocating them repeatedly. Forcing collections makes pauses worse, and the other options do not stop the allocation churn."),
  m("ca14", "csharp", "j",
    "```var tasks = new List<Task>();\nfor (int i = 0; i < 3; i++)\n    tasks.Add(Task.Run(() => Console.WriteLine(i)));\nawait Task.WhenAll(tasks);```\nWhat is a typical output?",
    ["0 1 2 in order",
     "0 1 2 in some order",
     "Always 2 2 2",
     "Often 3 3 3, because the lambdas capture one shared loop variable"],
    3,
    "A `for` loop declares a single `i` shared by all iterations, so lambdas that run after the loop ends see its final value 3. Copy it into a local inside the loop. A `foreach` variable is fresh per iteration since C# 5, which is why the two loops behave differently."),
  m("ca15", "csharp", "m",
    "A cache must allow only one async refresh at a time. A developer writes `lock (_gate) { data = await LoadAsync(); }` and gets a compiler error. What is the right approach?",
    ["Use `SemaphoreSlim(1, 1)` with `await _sem.WaitAsync()` and release it in `finally`",
     "Call `Monitor.Enter` and `Monitor.Exit` manually around the await",
     "Wrap the lock in `Task.Run` and call `.Wait()` on it",
     "Use `ReaderWriterLockSlim.EnterWriteLock` around the await"],
    0,
    "`lock` cannot contain `await` because `Monitor` locks are tied to a thread and the continuation may resume on another one. `SemaphoreSlim.WaitAsync` is the async-friendly mutual exclusion primitive. Manual `Monitor` calls, `Mutex` and `ReaderWriterLockSlim` all have the same thread-affinity problem."),
  m("ca16", "csharp", "s",
    "`static bool Less(IComparable a, IComparable b) => a.CompareTo(b) < 0;` is called millions of times with `int` arguments and the profiler shows heavy allocations. What fixes this without changing call sites?",
    ["Mark the parameters `in` so the ints are passed by reference",
     "Add `[MethodImpl(MethodImplOptions.AggressiveInlining)]` to the method",
     "Make it generic: `Less<T>(T a, T b) where T : IComparable<T>`",
     "Cast the arguments to `object` once before the loop"],
    2,
    "Converting an `int` to the non-generic `IComparable` boxes it, allocating on every call. A generic method constrained to `IComparable<T>` lets the JIT specialize for value types, so no boxing occurs and type inference keeps call sites unchanged. Inlining or `in` does not remove the conversion."),
  m("ca17", "csharp", "s",
    "On .NET 9 you change `private readonly object _gate = new();` to `private readonly Lock _gate = new();` and keep `lock (_gate) { ... }`. Which statement is accurate?",
    ["The block may now contain `await` because `Lock` is async-aware",
     "The `lock` statement uses `Lock.EnterScope()` instead of `Monitor`, but awaiting inside it is still not allowed",
     "It compiles to the same `Monitor.Enter` calls, so `Lock` is only a naming convention",
     "`Lock` is not reentrant, so a nested `lock (_gate)` on the same thread deadlocks"],
    1,
    "With C# 13 and `System.Threading.Lock`, the compiler pattern-binds `lock` to `EnterScope()`, giving a dedicated and cheaper lock type. It remains thread-affine, so `await` inside is still an error, and it supports recursive entry like `Monitor`."),
  m("ca18", "csharp", "j",
    "```catch (Exception ex)\n{\n    _log.LogError(ex, \"failed\");\n    throw ex;\n}```\nDevelopers complain that stack traces point to this catch block instead of the original failure. What is the fix?",
    ["Use `throw new Exception(ex.Message)` so the message is kept",
     "Add `[StackTraceHidden]` to the method",
     "Remove the logging call, which resets the stack trace",
     "Use `throw;` to rethrow with the original stack trace intact"],
    3,
    "`throw ex;` resets the stack trace to the current line, while a bare `throw;` rethrows the caught exception and preserves where it originated. Wrapping in a new `Exception` without passing the inner exception loses even more information."),
  m("ca19", "csharp", "s",
    "`var client = _cache.GetOrAdd(tenantId, id => CreateExpensiveClient(id));` uses a `ConcurrentDictionary`. During a burst for a new tenant, `CreateExpensiveClient` runs several times. Why?",
    ["Concurrent callers can each run the factory before one result wins; caching `Lazy<T>` values makes it run once",
     "`ConcurrentDictionary` locks only writes, so a stale read caused this framework bug",
     "`GetOrAdd` invokes the factory once per CPU core by design",
     "The delegate runs on every call; `GetOrAdd` caches only the key, not the value"],
    0,
    "`GetOrAdd` is thread-safe for the dictionary's state, but the value factory runs outside the lock, so racing threads may each execute it and only one value is stored. Storing `Lazy<T>` (or an async equivalent) and reading `.Value` ensures the expensive creation happens once."),
  m("ca20", "csharp", "m",
    "A check `role.ToLower() == \"admin\"` works everywhere except on a server using the `tr-TR` culture, where the role \"ADMIN\" fails. What is the correct fix?",
    ["Switch to `role.ToUpper() == \"ADMIN\"`, since upper-casing is not culture-sensitive",
     "Normalize both strings with `string.Normalize()` before comparing",
     "Use `string.Equals(role, \"admin\", StringComparison.OrdinalIgnoreCase)`",
     "Use `role.Contains(\"admin\", StringComparison.CurrentCultureIgnoreCase)`"],
    2,
    "In Turkish culture `\"ADMIN\".ToLower()` produces a dotless i, so the comparison fails; identifiers and protocol values should be compared with `OrdinalIgnoreCase`. `ToUpper()` is equally culture-sensitive, and the culture-aware comparison option keeps the same problem."),
  m("ca21", "csharp", "j",
    "An invoice total is computed with `double`: summing 0.1 ten times and comparing with 1.0 sometimes fails, and cents drift across thousands of lines. Which type should hold monetary amounts?",
    ["`float`, with a rounding call after each addition",
     "`decimal`, which stores base-10 fractions exactly within its precision",
     "`double`, compared using `Math.Abs(a - b) < double.Epsilon`",
     "`double` with `MidpointRounding.ToEven` configured globally"],
    1,
    "`double` and `float` are binary floating point, so values like 0.1 cannot be represented exactly and errors accumulate. `decimal` uses a base-10 representation suited to currency. `double.Epsilon` is the smallest positive denormal, not a useful comparison tolerance."),
  m("ca22", "csharp", "m",
    "Orders are stored with `DateTime.Now` by an app running in three regions. Reports show events out of order and duplicates around daylight-saving changes. Which design is correct?",
    ["Store `DateTime.Now` with `Kind` set to `Local` so each region's offset is preserved",
     "Run all servers in one chosen local time zone so `DateTime.Now` agrees",
     "Store `DateTime.Now.ToString(\"o\")` strings so the offset travels with the value",
     "Store UTC (`DateTime.UtcNow` or `DateTimeOffset.UtcNow`) and convert to the user's zone only for display"],
    3,
    "UTC is unambiguous and monotonic, so ordering and arithmetic stay correct; convert to local time at the edge. `DateTime.Kind` is not persisted by SQL Server column types, and a single local zone still has ambiguous and skipped hours at DST changes."),

  // ---------- ASP.NET Core ----------
  m("ca23", "aspnet", "m",
    "A global error-handling middleware is added after a custom `TenantMiddleware` in `Program.cs`. When `TenantMiddleware` throws, the client gets a raw 500 instead of your formatted error. Why?",
    ["Middleware only wraps components registered after it, so error handling must be added first in the pipeline",
     "Error middleware only catches exceptions thrown by controllers, not by other middleware",
     "Exceptions thrown before `UseRouting` are always swallowed by Kestrel",
     "Error-handling middleware must call `next()` twice, before and after logging"],
    0,
    "Each middleware wraps the ones that come after it via `next()`, so a try/catch or `UseExceptionHandler` only sees exceptions from later components. Register it at the very start of the pipeline. It is not limited to MVC code."),
  m("ca24", "aspnet", "j",
    "A user presents a valid JWT, but the token lacks the `Admin` role required by `[Authorize(Roles = \"Admin\")]`. Which status does ASP.NET Core return by default?",
    ["401 Unauthorized, since the request failed authorization",
     "404 Not Found, to hide that the endpoint exists",
     "403 Forbidden, since the caller is authenticated but not permitted",
     "400 Bad Request, because the token's claims do not match the policy"],
    2,
    "Authentication succeeded, so the failure is an authorization one and the JwtBearer handler responds with 403 Forbidden; 401 means the caller could not be authenticated at all. ASP.NET Core does not turn authorization failures into 404 unless you build that behavior."),
  m("ca25", "aspnet", "m",
    "Your API validates JWTs from the company identity provider. A token issued for the separate HR app, by the same issuer, is accepted by your API. Settings are `ValidateIssuer = true, ValidateAudience = false`. What should change?",
    ["Enable `ValidateLifetime` so tokens meant for other apps expire",
     "Enable `ValidateAudience` and set `ValidAudience` to this API's identifier",
     "Enable `RequireHttpsMetadata` so tokens are bound to the TLS session",
     "Rotate the signing key so tokens issued for other apps stop validating"],
    1,
    "The `aud` claim says which API a token is intended for; with audience validation off, any token from the trusted issuer works against your API. Rotating the key would break every app sharing that issuer, and lifetime or HTTPS settings do not bind a token to a particular audience."),
  m("ca26", "aspnet", "m",
    "A token expired two minutes ago, yet JwtBearer still accepts it. `ValidateLifetime` is left at its default and no custom validation exists. Why?",
    ["`ValidateLifetime` defaults to false",
     "`exp` is only checked when a refresh token is presented",
     "JwtBearer caches validated tokens for five minutes by default",
     "`TokenValidationParameters.ClockSkew` defaults to five minutes, extending the accepted lifetime"],
    3,
    "`ValidateLifetime` is true by default, but the default `ClockSkew` of 5 minutes is added to `exp`, so a recently expired token still passes. Set `ClockSkew` to a small value or zero if you need strict expiry. JwtBearer does not cache validation results."),
  m("ca27", "aspnet", "s",
    "A singleton `ReportScheduler` calls `_provider.GetRequiredService<PdfBuilder>()` for every job. `PdfBuilder` is registered transient and implements `IDisposable`. Memory grows steadily. Why?",
    ["The root provider tracks disposable transients it creates and holds them until shutdown; create a scope per job",
     "The container pools transient instances and grows the pool without limit",
     "The GC does not collect `IDisposable` objects until the caller invokes `Dispose`",
     "Each resolve adds a handler to the finalizer queue, which drains slowly"],
    0,
    "Resolving a disposable transient from the root provider makes the container keep a reference so it can dispose it at app shutdown, which defeats garbage collection. Resolve inside `CreateScope()` per job so the scope disposes it. Disposing manually does not remove the container's reference."),
  m("ca28", "aspnet", "m",
    "A singleton `PriceCache` takes the scoped `AppDbContext` in its constructor. In production (`ASPNETCORE_ENVIRONMENT=Production`) the app sometimes throws `A second operation was started on this context instance`. What is the root cause?",
    ["`AddDbContext` defaults to singleton lifetime in Production",
     "Connection pool exhaustion makes EF Core reuse a busy connection",
     "One scoped `DbContext` was captured by the singleton and is shared by concurrent requests, though it is not thread-safe",
     "SQL Server disallows concurrent queries from one application"],
    2,
    "A singleton holding a scoped service is a captive dependency: a single `DbContext` lives for the app's lifetime and gets used by parallel requests. Scope validation is on by default only in Development, so it passed silently here. Inject `IDbContextFactory<T>` or an `IServiceScopeFactory` instead."),
  m("ca29", "aspnet", "j",
    "A controller action takes `CreateUserDto` with `[Required] string Email`. Posting `{}` reaches the action with `Email == null` and a bad record is created. The controller has no `[ApiController]` attribute. What is the most idiomatic fix?",
    ["Add `[FromBody]` to the parameter so validation runs",
     "Add `[ApiController]` so invalid models automatically produce a 400 ValidationProblemDetails",
     "Call `TryValidateModel` in custom middleware before routing",
     "Change the DTO to a `record`, which validates required properties during binding"],
    1,
    "Validation runs without the attribute, but the action must check `ModelState.IsValid` itself; `[ApiController]` adds the automatic 400 response. `[FromBody]` changes where data comes from, not whether invalid data is rejected, and records do not validate attributes on their own."),
  m("ca30", "aspnet", "m",
    "You want every unhandled exception, from controllers and minimal APIs alike, turned into an `application/problem+json` response with a trace id, and `NotFoundException` mapped to 404 in one place. Which .NET 8+ approach fits?",
    ["Wrap each action body in try/catch and return `Problem()`",
     "Use `app.UseDeveloperExceptionPage()` in all environments",
     "Add a global `ExceptionFilterAttribute`, which also handles minimal API and middleware exceptions",
     "Register `AddProblemDetails()` and an `IExceptionHandler`, then call `UseExceptionHandler()`"],
    3,
    "`IExceptionHandler` implementations run from the exception handler middleware, which covers the entire pipeline, and `AddProblemDetails()` formats the response. MVC exception filters only see exceptions from controller actions, so they miss minimal APIs and other middleware."),
  m("ca31", "aspnet", "s",
    "You add `AddRateLimiter` with a fixed window of 100 requests per minute per user. After scaling from 1 to 4 pods, abusive users manage about 400 requests per minute. Why?",
    ["The built-in limiters keep their counters in process memory, so each instance enforces the limit independently",
     "Partitioned limiters multiply the limit by the number of CPU cores",
     "Kestrel applies the limit per listening socket rather than per user",
     "`UseRateLimiter` after `UseAuthorization` multiplies limits across replicas"],
    0,
    "The built-in rate limiting middleware has no shared store, so every replica counts separately and the effective limit scales with the instance count. Enforce it at a shared layer such as an API gateway or with a distributed counter in Redis."),
  m("ca32", "aspnet", "m",
    "A catalog endpoint is expensive. You want the server to cache responses even when clients send `Cache-Control: no-cache`, and to evict every entry tagged `products` after an admin update. Which feature fits?",
    ["Response caching middleware with `[ResponseCache(Duration = 60)]`",
     "`IMemoryCache` entries written inside the middleware with `Vary` headers",
     "Output caching (`AddOutputCache`, `CacheOutput`) with `IOutputCacheStore.EvictByTagAsync`",
     "A `Cache-Control: public, max-age=60` header so the CDN evicts by tag"],
    2,
    "Output caching is a server-side cache that you control, with policies and tag-based eviction. Response caching follows HTTP caching headers and respects client `no-cache` directives, and it has no tag eviction."),
  m("ca33", "aspnet", "s",
    "Users may edit only documents they own, and the owner is known only after loading the document. `[Authorize(Policy = \"CanEdit\")]` runs before the action body. How do you enforce ownership idiomatically?",
    ["Put the document ID in a JWT claim at sign-in and check it in the policy",
     "Load the document in the action, then call `IAuthorizationService.AuthorizeAsync(User, document, requirement)`",
     "Use a global filter that reads the document ID from the query string and loads it",
     "Assign an `Owner` role to the user and use `[Authorize(Roles = \"Owner\")]`"],
    1,
    "Resource-based authorization evaluates a requirement against the loaded resource using an authorization handler, which attribute-only policies cannot do because the resource is not available yet. Role checks cannot express per-document ownership."),
  m("ca34", "aspnet", "s",
    "Kubernetes pods restart in a loop whenever SQL Server has a short outage. Both liveness and readiness probes call `/health`, which includes a SQL check. What is the best change?",
    ["Increase the liveness probe's `failureThreshold` to 20",
     "Remove the SQL check so `/health` always reports Healthy",
     "Add a retry inside the health check so it never reports failure",
     "Expose a liveness endpoint with no dependency checks and a separate readiness endpoint, filtered by tags, that includes SQL"],
    3,
    "Liveness should only answer whether the process is stuck, because restarting pods will not fix a database outage; readiness decides whether to route traffic. Splitting them via `MapHealthChecks` with a `Predicate` keeps pods running but out of rotation. Higher thresholds only delay the loop."),
  m("ca35", "aspnet", "m",
    "A singleton `PricingService` reads `IOptions<PricingSettings>`. Operators edit `appsettings.json` with `reloadOnChange` enabled, but new values never appear until restart. What should be injected?",
    ["`IOptionsMonitor<PricingSettings>`, reading `CurrentValue`",
     "`IOptionsSnapshot<PricingSettings>`, which reloads on every change",
     "`IOptions<PricingSettings>` with `Configure` called again on each request",
     "`IConfiguration` bound once in the constructor with `Get<PricingSettings>()`"],
    0,
    "`IOptionsMonitor<T>` is a singleton that returns updated values and raises change notifications. `IOptionsSnapshot<T>` does reload per request, but it is scoped and cannot be injected into a singleton, and `IOptions<T>` is fixed after the first read."),
  m("ca36", "aspnet", "j",
    "An API creates `using var client = new HttpClient();` per request to call a partner. Under load it fails with `Address already in use` and many sockets sit in TIME_WAIT. What is the recommended fix?",
    ["Keep the `using` but call `client.Dispose()` explicitly before returning",
     "Set a shorter `client.Timeout` so sockets close faster",
     "Register a named or typed client with `AddHttpClient` and use `IHttpClientFactory`, which pools and rotates handlers",
     "Create one `HttpClient` per thread using `ThreadLocal`"],
    2,
    "Disposing an `HttpClient` closes the handler but the sockets linger in TIME_WAIT, so per-request clients exhaust ports. `IHttpClientFactory` reuses pooled handlers and recycles them periodically so DNS changes are still picked up."),
  m("ca37", "aspnet", "s",
    "A checkout service calls a payment API through a client configured with `AddStandardResilienceHandler()`. After a timeout, some customers are charged twice. Which fix is most appropriate?",
    ["Raise the attempt timeout so retries never fire",
     "Send an `Idempotency-Key` that the payment API deduplicates, and disable retries for unsafe methods like POST",
     "Add a circuit breaker so repeated charges stop after failures",
     "Lower `MaxRetryAttempts` to 1, which makes retries safe"],
    1,
    "Retrying a non-idempotent POST can repeat a side effect that actually succeeded before the timeout, so the operation needs an idempotency key or retries must be disabled for it. Fewer retries still risk a duplicate, and the standard handler already includes a circuit breaker."),
  m("ca38", "aspnet", "j",
    "`[HttpPost] Create(User user)` binds a JSON body straight to an EF entity that has `bool IsAdmin`. A tester posts `{\"name\":\"x\",\"isAdmin\":true}` and becomes an admin. What is the proper fix?",
    ["Add `[JsonIgnore]` on the controller parameter to hide `IsAdmin` in Swagger",
     "Put `[Required]` on `Name` so incomplete payloads are rejected",
     "Set `IsAdmin` to false in the entity constructor",
     "Bind to a request DTO containing only the allowed fields and map it to the entity"],
    3,
    "Binding directly to an entity lets clients set any property (over-posting or mass assignment). A purpose-built DTO whitelists what clients may send. Defaults in a constructor are overwritten by the binder, and hiding a field in documentation does not prevent it from being bound."),
  m("ca39", "aspnet", "m",
    "An Angular app at `https://app.example.com` calls `https://api.example.com` using cookies (`withCredentials: true`). The API policy is `AllowAnyOrigin().AllowAnyHeader().AllowAnyMethod().AllowCredentials()`. What happens?",
    ["ASP.NET Core rejects a wildcard origin combined with credentials; list explicit origins with `WithOrigins(...)`",
     "It works; `AllowCredentials` lets the browser send cookies safely to any origin",
     "The browser accepts it but ignores cookies on preflight `OPTIONS` requests only",
     "It works only when `UseCors` is placed after `UseAuthorization`"],
    0,
    "The CORS protocol forbids `Access-Control-Allow-Origin: *` together with credentials, and ASP.NET Core refuses to build that policy. Use `WithOrigins(\"https://app.example.com\")` with `AllowCredentials()`."),
  m("ca40", "aspnet", "s",
    "Behind an NGINX ingress that terminates TLS, the app's `Request.IsHttps` is false, redirects use `http://`, and the IP-based rate limiter sees one client IP for everyone. Which change addresses all three?",
    ["Call `app.UseHttpsRedirection()` earlier in the pipeline",
     "Read `X-Forwarded-For` by hand in each controller",
     "Configure `ForwardedHeadersOptions` (`XForwardedFor | XForwardedProto`) and call `UseForwardedHeaders()` first, trusting only the proxy",
     "Set `RequireHttpsMetadata = false` on the authentication handler"],
    2,
    "Kestrel only sees the proxy's connection, so the forwarded headers middleware must restore the original scheme and client IP; restrict `KnownProxies` or `KnownNetworks` so clients cannot spoof them. Moving the HTTPS redirect cannot fix the scheme the app sees."),
  m("ca41", "aspnet", "s",
    "An SPA moves from cookie-based sign-in to keeping a JWT in memory and sending it in an `Authorization: Bearer` header. How does this affect CSRF exposure on the API?",
    ["No change; browsers attach `Authorization` headers to cross-site form posts automatically",
     "Classic CSRF largely disappears because the browser does not add that header on its own, while cookie auth still needs antiforgery or SameSite protection",
     "It increases CSRF exposure because bearer tokens are readable by scripts",
     "CSRF no longer applies to any app, and XSS stops mattering as well"],
    1,
    "CSRF relies on the browser automatically attaching credentials such as cookies; a header set explicitly by your JavaScript is not sent by a forged cross-site request. Script-readable tokens raise XSS risk, a separate concern, and cookie-based apps still need antiforgery or SameSite."),
  m("ca42", "aspnet", "m",
    "```app.Use(async (ctx, next) => {\n    var sw = Stopwatch.StartNew();\n    await next();\n    ctx.Response.Headers[\"X-Elapsed\"] = sw.ElapsedMilliseconds.ToString();\n});```\nFor large responses this fails because the response has already started. What is the fix?",
    ["Call `ctx.Response.Body.Flush()` before setting the header",
     "Move the line before `await next()` and compute the time there",
     "Disable response buffering in Kestrel so headers can still be mutated",
     "Register the header in a `ctx.Response.OnStarting(...)` callback before calling `next()`"],
    3,
    "Headers are sent with the first body bytes, so they cannot change once the response has started. `OnStarting` runs just before that moment and is the supported place to add them. Moving the line earlier loses the elapsed time, and flushing only makes it worse."),
  m("ca43", "aspnet", "m",
    "You need to log the bound `CreateOrderRequest` argument and the matched action name, for MVC endpoints only. Why use an action filter rather than middleware?",
    ["Filters run inside the MVC pipeline after model binding, so they can see action arguments and `ActionDescriptor`, while middleware sees only the raw `HttpContext`",
     "Middleware cannot read request bodies, but filters can",
     "Filters run before routing, so they see every request including static files",
     "Middleware cannot be asynchronous, but filters can"],
    0,
    "Action filters wrap action execution and receive the bound arguments, `ModelState` and the action descriptor. Middleware runs for every request on `HttpContext` and knows nothing about bound parameters. Middleware can read bodies and be async."),
  m("ca44", "aspnet", "j",
    "Users close the browser during a 30-second report export, but the server keeps running the SQL query to completion. What is the simplest correct improvement?",
    ["Wrap the query in `Task.Run` so it can be abandoned when the client leaves",
     "Call `Thread.Abort` on the request thread from middleware",
     "Accept a `CancellationToken` parameter in the action and pass it to `ToListAsync(ct)` and other async calls",
     "Lower Kestrel's `RequestHeadersTimeout` to 5 seconds"],
    2,
    "A `CancellationToken` action parameter is bound to `HttpContext.RequestAborted`, which fires when the client disconnects; passing it down cancels the database command. `Task.Run` does not cancel anything, and `Thread.Abort` is not supported in .NET Core."),

  // ---------- Roadmap: C# and modern .NET ----------
  m("ca45", "rm-csharp", "s",
    "In C# 14 (.NET 10) you write `public string Name { get => field; set => field = value?.Trim() ?? \"\"; }` with no field declared. What does `field` refer to?",
    ["The property itself, so the setter recurses until a stack overflow",
     "The compiler-synthesized backing field of this property",
     "A reserved keyword for the type's first declared field",
     "A setter parameter equivalent to `value`"],
    1,
    "C# 14 field-backed properties let accessors use the contextual keyword `field` for the compiler-generated backing field, so you can add logic without declaring a private field. If a member already named `field` exists, write `@field` or `this.field` to refer to it."),
  m("ca46", "rm-csharp", "m",
    "With C# 14 you want a `list.IsEmpty` property available on every `IEnumerable<T>` without modifying the type. How?",
    ["Write `public static bool IsEmpty<T>(this IEnumerable<T> s)` and call it without parentheses",
     "Add a `partial` extension property on `IEnumerable<T>` in a partial class",
     "Add a default interface member to `IEnumerable<T>`",
     "Declare an `extension<T>(IEnumerable<T> source)` block containing a `bool IsEmpty` property"],
    3,
    "C# 14 introduces extension members: an `extension` block can declare extension properties and static members, which classic `this` extension methods could not. Extension methods always need parentheses, and you cannot add members to framework interfaces."),
  m("ca47", "rm-csharp", "m",
    "```public class OrderService(IOrderRepo repo, ILogger<OrderService> log)\n{\n    public Task<Order?> Get(int id) => repo.FindAsync(id);\n}```\nAnother class writes `service.repo`. What happens?",
    ["Compile error: for a class, primary constructor parameters are not public members; only records generate properties",
     "It compiles; the parameters become public init-only properties",
     "It compiles; the parameters become `internal readonly` fields visible within the assembly",
     "Compile error: primary constructors are allowed only on records and structs"],
    0,
    "Since C# 12, classes and structs can have primary constructors, but their parameters are plain captured values (the compiler creates hidden fields only for those you use), not accessible members. Records are the type that synthesizes public properties from the parameter list."),
  m("ca48", "rm-csharp", "j",
    "```int[] first = [1, 2];\nint[] second = [3, 4];\nList<int> all = [..first, ..second, 5];\nfirst[0] = 99;```\nWhat does `all` contain afterwards?",
    ["99, 2, 3, 4, 5, because spread keeps references to the source arrays",
     "A lazy view over both arrays that reflects later changes",
     "1, 2, 3, 4, 5, because spread copies the elements into the new list",
     "Nothing; this is a compile error because spread cannot mix arrays with `List<int>`"],
    2,
    "A collection expression builds a new collection and the spread element copies the source items into it, so later changes to `first` are not reflected. It is not a lazy `Concat`, and spreading works across collection types."),
  m("ca49", "rm-csharp", "m",
    "A minimal API handler returns `Results.Ok(dto)` or `Results.NotFound()` as `Task<IResult>`, and the generated OpenAPI document shows no response schema. Which change lets metadata be inferred from the return type?",
    ["Add `[ApiController]` to the lambda",
     "Return `TypedResults.Ok(dto)` and declare `Task<Results<Ok<Dto>, NotFound>>`",
     "Change the return type to `object`",
     "Move the endpoint into a controller, because minimal APIs cannot describe responses"],
    1,
    "`TypedResults` and the `Results<...>` union expose the possible response types and status codes to the compiler and OpenAPI generator, and make unit tests easier. Plain `IResult` hides them, so you would need `.Produces<T>()`; minimal APIs can describe responses fine."),
  m("ca50", "rm-csharp", "m",
    "On .NET 8, a minimal API handler takes `CreateUser dto` whose `Email` has `[Required]` and `[EmailAddress]`. A POST of `{}` still runs the handler with a null `Email`. Which statement is true?",
    ["Minimal APIs validate DataAnnotations automatically, so a configuration error must exist",
     "Decorating the lambda parameter with `[FromBody]` triggers DataAnnotations validation",
     "Adding `[ApiController]` to the containing static class enables validation",
     "The framework does not evaluate the attributes; validate explicitly, for example in an endpoint filter using `Validator.TryValidateObject` or FluentValidation"],
    3,
    "The automatic 400 response comes from MVC's `[ApiController]` behavior, which minimal APIs do not have in .NET 8. You must run validation yourself, commonly in a reusable endpoint filter, and return `Results.ValidationProblem`."),
  m("ca51", "rm-csharp", "m",
    "`StripeGateway` and `AdyenGateway` both implement `IPaymentGateway`. `CheckoutService` needs Stripe and `RefundService` needs Adyen, but plain injection always receives the last registration. What fixes this on .NET 8+ without a factory class?",
    ["Register with `AddKeyedScoped<IPaymentGateway, StripeGateway>(\"stripe\")` and inject with `[FromKeyedServices(\"stripe\")]`",
     "Register both normally and take `IEnumerable<IPaymentGateway>.First()` in each consumer",
     "Add `[Named(\"stripe\")]` to the constructor parameter",
     "Register one as scoped and the other as singleton so the container picks by lifetime"],
    0,
    ".NET 8 added keyed services: register each implementation with a key and request it with `[FromKeyedServices(key)]`. Taking the first item of an enumerable depends on registration order and does not express intent, and the container never chooses by lifetime."),
  m("ca52", "rm-csharp", "s",
    "You must call a rate-sensitive partner API for 5,000 IDs with at most 10 requests in flight. `await Task.WhenAll(ids.Select(CallAsync))` overwhelms it. Which approach is correct?",
    ["`Parallel.ForEach(ids, new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 10 }, async id => await CallAsync(id))`",
     "`ids.AsParallel().WithDegreeOfParallelism(10).Select(id => CallAsync(id))`",
     "`await Parallel.ForEachAsync(ids, new ParallelOptions { MaxDegreeOfParallelism = 10 }, async (id, ct) => await CallAsync(id, ct))`",
     "Wrap each call in `Task.Run` and call `ThreadPool.SetMaxThreads(10, 10)`"],
    2,
    "`Parallel.ForEachAsync` (.NET 6+) is designed for asynchronous bodies and limits concurrency to `MaxDegreeOfParallelism`. With `Parallel.ForEach`, an async lambda becomes `async void`, so the loop finishes without waiting for the calls; PLINQ likewise does not await tasks."),

  // ---------- Roadmap: backend fundamentals ----------
  m("ca53", "rm-backend", "s",
    "Your API runs as four replicas and calls `db.Database.Migrate()` in `Program.cs`. Deploys occasionally fail with schema errors, and the app's SQL login needs `db_owner`. What is the recommended production approach?",
    ["Replace `Migrate()` with `EnsureCreated()`, which is safe for concurrent startup",
     "Generate an idempotent SQL script or migration bundle and run it once as a deployment step with its own credentials",
     "Keep `Migrate()` but add a random startup delay to each replica",
     "Run `Migrate()` only on the pod whose hostname ends in `-0`"],
    1,
    "Microsoft recommends applying migrations from a script or bundle in the deployment pipeline, so one process with elevated rights changes the schema and the app runs with least privilege. `EnsureCreated()` bypasses migrations entirely, and startup tricks remain racy."),
  m("ca54", "rm-backend", "m",
    "Two support agents open the same `Ticket`, both edit it, and the second save silently overwrites the first agent's changes. Which EF Core mechanism detects this?",
    ["Wrap `SaveChangesAsync` in a `Serializable` transaction",
     "Use `AsNoTracking()` on the load query so stale entities are rejected",
     "Put a `lock` around `SaveChangesAsync` in the controller",
     "Add a `rowversion` concurrency token (`[Timestamp]` or `IsRowVersion()`) and catch `DbUpdateConcurrencyException`"],
    3,
    "With a concurrency token, EF adds it to the `WHERE` clause of the UPDATE, and zero affected rows raises `DbUpdateConcurrencyException`, which you handle by reloading or merging. Transactions and locks only cover the brief save, not the time between the user's read and write, and an in-process lock fails across instances."),
  m("ca55", "rm-backend", "j",
    "```var sql = $\"SELECT * FROM Users WHERE Email = '{email}'\";\nvar u = await conn.QueryFirstOrDefaultAsync<User>(sql);```\nA security review flags this. What is the correct fix with Dapper?",
    ["Use a parameter: `\"... WHERE Email = @email\"` with `new { email }`",
     "Escape single quotes with `email.Replace(\"'\", \"''\")` before interpolating",
     "Pass a `FormattableString` so Dapper parameterizes the interpolation automatically",
     "Switch to `QueryAsync`, since `QueryFirstOrDefaultAsync` allows injection"],
    0,
    "Parameters send the value separately from the SQL text, which removes injection by construction. Manual escaping is easy to get wrong, and unlike EF Core's `FromSql`, Dapper does not turn interpolated strings into parameters."),
  m("ca56", "rm-backend", "m",
    "`SELECT * FROM Orders WHERE YEAR(OrderDate) = 2025` is slow even though `OrderDate` is indexed, and the plan shows an index scan. What is the best fix?",
    ["Add `WITH (FORCESEEK)` to the table reference",
     "Add `OPTION (RECOMPILE)` so a new plan is built for 2025",
     "Rewrite it as `OrderDate >= '2025-01-01' AND OrderDate < '2026-01-01'`",
     "Update statistics, because stale statistics cause scans"],
    2,
    "Wrapping the column in a function makes the predicate non-sargable, so SQL Server must evaluate it for every row; a range on the bare column lets it seek the index. Hints and fresh statistics do not change that the predicate cannot be used for a seek."),
  m("ca57", "rm-backend", "s",
    "A reporting query on SQL Server under default READ COMMITTED isolation blocks behind long write transactions, and the team proposes `WITH (NOLOCK)`. Which option gives consistent reads without readers blocking on writers?",
    ["Raise isolation to `SERIALIZABLE` for the report session",
     "Enable `READ_COMMITTED_SNAPSHOT` on the database so readers use row versions instead of shared locks",
     "Use `NOLOCK`, which reads only committed rows without taking locks",
     "Use `REPEATABLE READ`, which releases shared locks immediately"],
    1,
    "Read committed snapshot isolation (RCSI) serves readers the last committed row version from tempdb, so they neither block nor are blocked by writers. `NOLOCK` is `READ UNCOMMITTED`, which can return dirty, duplicated or missing rows, and stronger isolation levels increase blocking."),
  m("ca58", "rm-backend", "s",
    "Security requires that a stolen JWT stops working within minutes and that users can sign out everywhere. The API is otherwise stateless. Which design meets this?",
    ["Issue 24-hour JWTs and delete the token from client storage on sign-out",
     "Rotate the signing key on every sign-out so all outstanding tokens fail",
     "Keep long-lived JWTs but validate `exp` with `ClockSkew = TimeSpan.Zero`",
     "Issue short-lived access tokens (about 5 to 15 minutes) plus a rotating refresh token stored hashed server-side that can be revoked"],
    3,
    "A short access-token lifetime bounds the damage of theft, and revoking the server-side refresh token stops renewal, which is what makes sign-out effective. Deleting a client copy does not invalidate a stolen token, and rotating the key logs out every user."),
  m("ca59", "rm-backend", "s",
    "A hot product key in Redis has a 60-second TTL. When it expires, 400 concurrent requests all miss and hit SQL Server, spiking CPU. What mitigates this?",
    ["Let only one caller per key rebuild the value while others wait for it, using a per-key lock or `HybridCache.GetOrCreateAsync`",
     "Raise the TTL from 60 seconds to 24 hours so expiry happens less often",
     "Add retry with exponential backoff to the SQL query",
     "Use a Redis pipeline so the 400 reads are sent together"],
    0,
    "This is a cache stampede: expiry makes many callers recompute at once. Single-flight loading (a per-key semaphore, or `HybridCache`, which coalesces concurrent requests) lets one query repopulate the cache. A longer TTL only postpones the same spike and serves staler data."),
  m("ca60", "rm-backend", "m",
    "A `BackgroundService` polls a queue. After a transient `SqlException` escapes `ExecuteAsync`, the entire API process shuts down on .NET 8. What explains it and what is the fix?",
    ["The exception is swallowed and the loop stops silently; add a health check",
     "Kestrel treats worker exceptions as bad requests; add `UseExceptionHandler`",
     "The default `BackgroundServiceExceptionBehavior` is `StopHost`; catch and log errors inside the loop (excluding `OperationCanceledException`) and retry",
     "`IHostedService` must run in a separate process; move it to a worker container"],
    2,
    "Since .NET 6 an unhandled exception from `ExecuteAsync` stops the host by default. Handle expected failures inside the polling loop and keep going. The quiet-stop behavior described in the first option was the older .NET 5 behavior."),
];
